scenarios.prompt:
I have this MobX action:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  this.items = items;
  this.loading = false;
}
```

What needs to change so it works correctly under MobX's enforceActions?

calibration.known_right:
The problem is that everything after the `await` runs outside the action's original transaction, so MobX no longer treats those assignments as happening inside an action -- under enforceActions that throws, and even without it the reaction system can miss the update. Wrap the post-await mutations in runInAction:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  runInAction(() => {
    this.loading = true;
  });
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

Alternatively, rewrite it as a `flow` generator, which keeps the whole body inside one action and needs no runInAction at all:

```ts
fetchItems = flow(function* (this: ItemStore) {
  this.loading = true;
  this.items = yield this.service.getAll();
  this.loading = false;
});
```

Either way, re-run the type-check and confirm no enforceActions warning/throw fires when this action runs.

scenarios.prompt:
This component reads `store.items.length` in its JSX but never updates when items change:

```tsx
function ItemList({ store }: { store: ItemStore }) {
  return <div>{store.items.length} items</div>;
}
```

What's wrong and how do I fix it?

calibration.known_right:
ItemList reads `store.items.length` directly, but it's a plain function component -- nothing tells MobX to track its render and re-run it when items changes. Wrap ItemList itself in observer from mobx-react-lite:

```tsx
import { observer } from "mobx-react-lite";

const ItemList = observer(({ store }: { store: ItemStore }) => (
  <div>{store.items.length} items</div>
));
```

observer makes MobX track every observable read during that component's render and re-render it specifically when one of those observables changes. Since ItemList is the component doing the read, it has to be the one wrapped -- wrapping some ancestor further up the tree would not make ItemList itself re-render on its own.
